\f0\b\fs36 \cf0 Introduction[{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://en.wikipedia.org/w/index.php?title=Gravitational_wave&action=edit&section=1"}}{\fldrslt \cf2 \ul \ulc0 edit}}]
\f1\b0\fs24 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sb240\sa240\partightenfactor0
\cf0 In {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Albert_Einstein"}}{\fldrslt \cf2 \ul \ulc0 Einstein's}} theory of {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/General_relativity"}}{\fldrslt \cf2 \ul \ulc0 general relativity}}, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Gravity"}}{\fldrslt \cf2 \ul \ulc0 gravity}} is treated as a phenomenon resulting from the curvature of {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Spacetime"}}{\fldrslt \cf2 \ul \ulc0 spacetime}}. This curvature is caused by the presence of {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Stress%E2%80%93energy_tensor"}}{\fldrslt \cf2 \ul \ulc0 mass}}. Generally, the more mass that is contained within a given volume of space, the greater the curvature of spacetime will be at the boundary of its volume.{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Gravitational_wave#cite_note-HTUW-14"}}{\fldrslt \cf2 \super \ul \ulc0 [}}\cf2 \super \ul \ulc0 14]\cf0 \nosupersub \ulnone  As objects with mass move around in spacetime, the curvature changes to reflect the changed locations of those objects. In {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Gravitational_wave#Sources"}}{\fldrslt \cf2 \ul certain circumstances}}, accelerating objects generate changes in this curvature, which propagate outwards at the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Speed_of_light"}}{\fldrslt \cf2 \ul speed of light}} in a wave-like manner. These propagating phenomena are known as gravitational waves.\
As a gravitational wave passes an observer, that observer will find spacetime distorted by the effects of {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Strain_(materials_science)"}}{\fldrslt \cf2 \ul strain}}. Distances between objects increase and decrease rhythmically as the wave passes, at a frequency corresponding to that of the wave. This occurs despite such free objects never being subjected to an unbalanced {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Force"}}{\fldrslt \cf2 \ul force}}. The magnitude of this effect decreases proportional to the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Inversely_proportional"}}{\fldrslt \cf2 \ul inverse}} distance from the source.{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Gravitational_wave#cite_note-Schutz2009-15"}}{\fldrslt \cf2 \super \ul [}}\cf2 \super \ul 15]\cf0 \ulnone :227\nosupersub  Inspiraling {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Binary_neutron_stars"}}{\fldrslt \cf2 \ul binary neutron stars}} are predicted to be a powerful source of gravitational waves as they coalesce, due to the very large acceleration of their masses as they {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Orbit"}}{\fldrslt \cf2 \ul orbit}} close to one another. However, due to the astronomical distances to these sources, the effects when measured on Earth are predicted to be very small, having strains of less than 1 part in 10\super 20\nosupersub . Scientists have demonstrated the existence of these waves with ever more sensitive detectors. The most sensitive detector accomplished the task possessing a sensitivity measurement of about one part in 7022499999999999999\uc0\u9824 5\'d710\super 22\nosupersub  (as of 2012{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://en.wikipedia.org/w/index.php?title=Gravitational_wave&action=edit"}}{\fldrslt \cf2 \super \ul [update]}}) provided by the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/LIGO"}}{\fldrslt \cf2 \ul LIGO}} and {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/VIRGO"}}{\fldrslt \cf2 \ul VIRGO}} observatories.{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Gravitational_wave#cite_note-16"}}{\fldrslt \cf2 \super \ul [}}\cf2 \super \ul 16]\cf0 \nosupersub \ulnone  A space based observatory, the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Laser_Interferometer_Space_Antenna"}}{\fldrslt \cf2 \ul Laser Interferometer Space Antenna}}, is currently under development by {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/European_Space_Agency"}}{\fldrslt \cf2 \ul ESA}}.\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf0  \
Linearly polarised gravitational wave\
\pard\pardeftab720\sb240\sa240\partightenfactor0
\cf0 Gravitational waves can penetrate regions of space that electromagnetic waves cannot. They are able to allow the observation of the merger of black holes and possibly other exotic objects in the distant Universe. Such systems cannot be observed with more traditional means such as {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Optical_telescope"}}{\fldrslt \cf2 \ul optical telescopes}} or {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Radio_telescope"}}{\fldrslt \cf2 \ul radio telescopes}}, and so {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Gravitational-wave_astronomy"}}{\fldrslt \cf2 \ul gravitational-wave astronomy}} gives new insights into the working of the Universe. In particular, gravitational waves could be of interest to cosmologists as they offer a possible way of observing the very early Universe. This is not possible with conventional astronomy, since before {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Recombination_(cosmology)"}}{\fldrslt \cf2 \ul recombination}} the Universe was opaque to electromagnetic radiation.{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Gravitational_wave#cite_note-17"}}{\fldrslt \cf2 \super \ul [}}\cf2 \super \ul 17]\cf0 \nosupersub \ulnone  Precise measurements of gravitational waves will also allow scientists to test more thoroughly the general theory of relativity.\
In principle, gravitational waves could exist at any frequency. However, very low frequency waves would be impossible to detect and there is no credible source for detectable waves of very high frequency. {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Stephen_Hawking"}}{\fldrslt \cf2 \ul Stephen Hawking}} and {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Werner_Israel"}}{\fldrslt \cf2 \ul Werner Israel}} list different frequency bands for gravitational waves that could plausibly be detected, ranging from 10\super \uc0\u8722 7\nosupersub  Hz up to 10\super 11\nosupersub  Hz.{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Gravitational_wave#cite_note-HI-18"}}{\fldrslt \cf2 \super \ul [}}\cf2 \super \ul 18]\cf0 \nosupersub \ulnone \
\pard\pardeftab720\sb320\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f0\b\fs36 \cf0 History[{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://en.wikipedia.org/w/index.php?title=Gravitational_wave&action=edit&section=2"}}{\fldrslt \cf2 \ul edit}}]
\f1\b0\fs24 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf0  \
Primordial gravitational waves are hypothesized to arise from {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Inflation_(cosmology)"}}{\fldrslt \cf2 \ul cosmic inflation}}, a {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Faster-than-light#Universal_expansion"}}{\fldrslt \cf2 \ul faster-than-light}} expansion just after the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Big_Bang"}}{\fldrslt \cf2 \ul Big Bang}} (2014).{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Gravitational_wave#cite_note-BICEP2-2014-19"}}{\fldrslt \cf2 \super \ul [}}\cf2 \super \ul 19]{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Gravitational_wave#cite_note-NASA-20140317-20"}}{\fldrslt [}}20]{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Gravitational_wave#cite_note-NYT-20140317-21"}}{\fldrslt [}}21]\cf0 \nosupersub \ulnone \
\pard\pardeftab720\sb240\sa240\partightenfactor0
\cf0 In 1905, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Henri_Poincar%C3%A9"}}{\fldrslt \cf2 \ul Henri Poincar\'e9}} first suggested that in analogy to an accelerating {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Electrical_charge"}}{\fldrslt \cf2 \ul electrical charge}} producing {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Electromagnetic_wave"}}{\fldrslt \cf2 \ul electromagnetic waves}}, accelerated masses in a relativistic field theory of gravity should produce gravitational waves.{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Gravitational_wave#cite_note-22"}}{\fldrslt \cf2 \super \ul [}}\cf2 \super \ul 22]{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Gravitational_wave#cite_note-CGShistory-23"}}{\fldrslt [}}23]\cf0 \nosupersub \ulnone  When Einstein published his {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/General_relativity"}}{\fldrslt \cf2 \ul theory of general relativity}} in 1915, he was skeptical of Poincar\'e9's idea since the theory implied there were no "gravitational dipoles". Nonetheless, he still pursued the idea and based on various approximations came to the conclusion there must, in fact, be three types of gravitational wave (dubbed longitudinal-longitudinal, transverse-longitudinal, and transverse-transverse by {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hermann_Weyl"}}{\fldrslt \cf2 \ul Hermann Weyl}}).{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Gravitational_wave#cite_note-CGShistory-23"}}{\fldrslt \cf2 \super \ul [}}\cf2 \super \ul 23]\cf0 \nosupersub \ulnone \
However, the nature of Einstein's approximations led many (including Einstein himself) to doubt the result. In 1922, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Arthur_Eddington"}}{\fldrslt \cf2 \ul Arthur Eddington}} showed that two of Einstein's types of waves were artifacts of the coordinate system he used, and could be made to propagate at any speed by choosing appropriate coordinates, leading Eddington to jest that they "propagate at the speed of thought".{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Gravitational_wave#cite_note-Kennefick2016-24"}}{\fldrslt \cf2 \super \ul [}}\cf2 \super \ul 24]\cf0 \ulnone :72\nosupersub  This also cast doubt on the physicality of the third (transverse-transverse) type (which Eddington showed always propagate at the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Speed_of_light"}}{\fldrslt \cf2 \ul speed of light}} regardless of coordinate system). In 1936, Einstein and {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Nathan_Rosen"}}{\fldrslt \cf2 \ul Nathan Rosen}} submitted a paper to {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Physical_Review"}}{\fldrslt \cf2 \ul Physical Review}} in which they claimed gravitational waves could not exist in the full theory of general relativity because any such solution of the field equations would have a singularity. The journal sent their manuscript to be reviewed by {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Howard_P._Robertson"}}{\fldrslt \cf2 \ul Howard P. Robertson}}, who (anonymously) reported that the singularities in question were simply the harmless coordinate singularities of the employed cylindrical coordinates. Einstein, who was unfamiliar with the concept of peer review, angrily withdrew the manuscript, never to publish in Physical Review again. Nonetheless, his assistant {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Leopold_Infeld"}}{\fldrslt \cf2 \ul Leopold Infeld}}, who had been in contact with Robertson, convinced Einstein that the criticism was correct and the paper was rewritten with the opposite conclusion (and published elsewhere).{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Gravitational_wave#cite_note-CGShistory-23"}}{\fldrslt \cf2 \super \ul [}}\cf2 \super \ul 23]{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Gravitational_wave#cite_note-Kennefick2016-24"}}{\fldrslt [}}24]\cf0 \ulnone :79ff\nosupersub \
In 1956, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Felix_Pirani"}}{\fldrslt \cf2 \ul Felix Pirani}} remedied the confusion caused by the use of various coordinate systems by rephrasing the gravitational waves in terms of the manifestly observable {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Riemann_curvature_tensor"}}{\fldrslt \cf2 \ul Riemann curvature tensor}}. At the time this work was mostly ignored because the community was focused on a different question: whether gravitational waves could transmit {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Energy"}}{\fldrslt \cf2 \ul energy}}. This matter was settled by a thought experiment proposed by {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Richard_Feynman"}}{\fldrslt \cf2 \ul Richard Feynman}} during the first "GR" conference at {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/University_of_North_Carolina_at_Chapel_Hill"}}{\fldrslt \cf2 \ul Chapel Hill}} in 1957. In short, his argument (known as the "{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sticky_bead_argument"}}{\fldrslt \cf2 \ul sticky bead argument}}") notes that if one takes a stick with beads then the effect of a passing gravitational wave would be to move the beads along the stick; friction would then produce heat, implying that the passing wave had done {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Work_(physics)"}}{\fldrslt \cf2 \ul work}}. Shortly after, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hermann_Bondi"}}{\fldrslt \cf2 \ul Hermann Bondi}} (a former gravitational wave skeptic) published a detailed version of the "sticky bead argument".{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Gravitational_wave#cite_note-CGShistory-23"}}{\fldrslt \cf2 \super \ul [}}\cf2 \super \ul 23]\cf0 \nosupersub \ulnone \
After the Chapel Hill conference, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Joseph_Weber"}}{\fldrslt \cf2 \ul Joseph Weber}} started designing and building the first gravitational wave detectors now known as {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Weber_bar"}}{\fldrslt \cf2 \ul Weber bars}}. In 1969, Weber claimed to have detected the first gravitational waves and by 1970 he was "detecting" signals regularly from the center of {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Milky_Way"}}{\fldrslt \cf2 \ul Milky Way}}; however, the frequency of detection soon raised doubts on the validity of his observations as the implied rate of energy loss of the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Milky_Way"}}{\fldrslt \cf2 \ul Milky Way}} would drain our galaxy of energy on a timescale much shorter than its inferred age. These doubts were strengthened when, by the mid-1970s, repeat experiments from other groups building their own Weber bars across the globe failed to find any signals, and by the late 1970s general consensus was that Weber's results were spurious.{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Gravitational_wave#cite_note-CGShistory-23"}}{\fldrslt \cf2 \super \ul [}}\cf2 \super \ul 23]\cf0 \nosupersub \ulnone \
In the same period, the first indirect evidence for the existence of gravitational waves was discovered. In 1974, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Russell_Alan_Hulse"}}{\fldrslt \cf2 \ul Russell Alan Hulse}} and {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Joseph_Hooton_Taylor,_Jr."}}{\fldrslt \cf2 \ul Joseph Hooton Taylor, Jr.}} discovered the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hulse-Taylor_binary"}}{\fldrslt \cf2 \ul first binary pulsar}} (a discovery that earned them the 1993 {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Nobel_Prize_in_Physics"}}{\fldrslt \cf2 \ul Nobel Prize in Physics}}). In 1979, results were published detailing measurement of the gradual decay of the orbital period of the Hulse-Taylor pulsar, which fitted precisely with the loss of energy and angular momentum in gravitational radiation predicted by general relativity.{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Gravitational_wave#cite_note-CGShistory-23"}}{\fldrslt \cf2 \super \ul [}}\cf2 \super \ul 23]\cf0 \nosupersub \ulnone \
\pard\pardeftab720\li720\fi-360\sb240\partightenfactor0
\ls1\ilvl0\cf0 		1962 \'96 M. E. Gertsenshtein and V. I. Pustovoit publish the first paper describing the principles for using interferometers to detect very long wavelength gravitational waves.{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Gravitational_wave#cite_note-25"}}{\fldrslt \cf2 \super \ul [}}\cf2 \super \ul 25]\cf0 \nosupersub \ulnone \
\pard\pardeftab720\li720\fi-360\partightenfactor0
\ls1\ilvl0\cf0 		1984 \'96 {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Kip_Thorne"}}{\fldrslt \cf2 \ul Kip Thorne}}, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ronald_Drever"}}{\fldrslt \cf2 \ul Ronald Drever}}, and {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rainer_Weiss"}}{\fldrslt \cf2 \ul Rainer Weiss}} form a steering committee after the NSF asks {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Massachusetts_Institute_of_Technology"}}{\fldrslt \cf2 \ul MIT}} and {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/California_Institute_of_Technology"}}{\fldrslt \cf2 \ul Caltech}} to join forces to lead a LIGO project.{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Gravitational_wave#cite_note-quantamag-26"}}{\fldrslt \cf2 \super \ul [}}\cf2 \super \ul 26]{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Gravitational_wave#cite_note-Committee_on_Setting_Priorities_for_NSF-Sponsored_Large_Research_Facility_Projects.2C_Committee_on_Science.2C_Engineering.2C_and_Public_Policy.2C_Policy_and_Global_Affairs.2C_Board_on_Physics_and_Astronomy.2C_Division_on_Engineering_and_Physical_Sciences.2C_National_Research_Council._2004_109.E2.80.93117-27"}}{\fldrslt [}}27]\cf0 \nosupersub \ulnone \
		1994 \'96 LIGO Laboratory Director {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Barry_Barish"}}{\fldrslt \cf2 \ul Barry Barish}} and his team create the LIGO study, project plan, and budget, receive long-withheld NSF funding, and go-ahead for construction. Barish is appointed Principal Investigator and LIGO, with a budget of US$395 million, becomes the largest overall funded NSF project in history.{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Gravitational_wave#cite_note-Committee_on_Setting_Priorities_for_NSF-Sponsored_Large_Research_Facility_Projects.2C_Committee_on_Science.2C_Engineering.2C_and_Public_Policy.2C_Policy_and_Global_Affairs.2C_Board_on_Physics_and_Astronomy.2C_Division_on_Engineering_and_Physical_Sciences.2C_National_Research_Council._2004_109.E2.80.93117-27"}}{\fldrslt \cf2 \super \ul [}}\cf2 \super \ul 27]\cf0 \nosupersub \ulnone \
		1997 \'96 The {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/LIGO_Scientific_Collaboration"}}{\fldrslt \cf2 \ul LIGO Scientific Collaboration (LSC)}} and the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Gravitational_Wave_International_Committee"}}{\fldrslt \cf2 \ul Gravitational Wave International Committee (GWIC)}} are formed.{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Gravitational_wave#cite_note-28"}}{\fldrslt \cf2 \super \ul [}}\cf2 \super \ul 28]{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Gravitational_wave#cite_note-29"}}{\fldrslt [}}29]\cf0 \nosupersub \ulnone \
		2002 \'96 LIGO begins the search for gravitational waves{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Gravitational_wave#cite_note-30"}}{\fldrslt \cf2 \super \ul [}}\cf2 \super \ul 30]\cf0 \nosupersub \ulnone \
		2004 \'96 Advanced LIGO upgrade is approved by the National Science Board.{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Gravitational_wave#cite_note-31"}}{\fldrslt \cf2 \super \ul [}}\cf2 \super \ul 31]\cf0 \nosupersub \ulnone \
		2005 - The binary black hole problem solved: three groups independently developed groundbreaking new methods to model the inspiral, merger, and ring-down of binary black holes.{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Gravitational_wave#cite_note-Pretorius2005-32"}}{\fldrslt \cf2 \super \ul [}}\cf2 \super \ul 32]{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Gravitational_wave#cite_note-CampanelliLousto2006-33"}}{\fldrslt [}}33]{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Gravitational_wave#cite_note-BakerCentrella2006-34"}}{\fldrslt [}}34]\cf0 \nosupersub \ulnone \
		2014 \'96 Astronomers at the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Harvard%E2%80%93Smithsonian_Center_for_Astrophysics"}}{\fldrslt \cf2 \ul Harvard\'96Smithsonian Center for Astrophysics}} erroneously claim that they have detected and produced "the first direct image of gravitational waves" in the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cosmic_microwave_background"}}{\fldrslt \cf2 \ul cosmic microwave background}}.{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Gravitational_wave#cite_note-BICEP2-2014-19"}}{\fldrslt \cf2 \super \ul [}}\cf2 \super \ul 19]{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Gravitational_wave#cite_note-NASA-20140317-20"}}{\fldrslt [}}20]{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Gravitational_wave#cite_note-35"}}{\fldrslt [}}35]{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Gravitational_wave#cite_note-36"}}{\fldrslt [}}36]\cf0 \nosupersub \ulnone \
		2015 \'96 Advanced LIGO begins operation.\
