\cf0 In general terms, gravitational waves are radiated by objects whose motion involves acceleration and its change, provided that the motion is not perfectly spherically {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Symmetric"}}{\fldrslt \cf2 \ul symmetric}} (like an expanding or contracting sphere) or rotationally symmetric (like a spinning disk or sphere). A simple example of this principle is a spinning {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dumbbell"}}{\fldrslt \cf2 \ul dumbbell}}. If the dumbbell spins around its axis of symmetry, it will not radiate gravitational waves; if it tumbles end over end, as in the case of two planets orbiting each other, it will radiate gravitational waves. The heavier the dumbbell, and the faster it tumbles, the greater is the gravitational radiation it will give off. In an extreme case, such as when the two weights of the dumbbell are massive stars like neutron stars or black holes, orbiting each other quickly, then significant amounts of gravitational radiation would be given off.\
Some more detailed examples:\
\pard\pardeftab720\li720\fi-360\sb240\partightenfactor0
\ls3\ilvl0\cf0 		Two objects orbiting each other, as a planet would orbit the Sun, 
\f3\i will
\f1\i0  radiate.\
\pard\pardeftab720\li720\fi-360\partightenfactor0
\ls3\ilvl0\cf0 		A spinning non-axisymmetric planetoid \'97 say with a large bump or dimple on the equator \'97 
\f3\i will
\f1\i0  radiate.\
		A {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Supernova"}}{\fldrslt \cf2 \ul supernova}} 
\f3\i will
\f1\i0  radiate except in the unlikely event that the explosion is perfectly symmetric.\
		An isolated non-spinning solid object moving at a constant velocity 
\f3\i will not
\f1\i0  radiate. This can be regarded as a consequence of the principle of {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Law_of_conservation_of_linear_momentum"}}{\fldrslt \cf2 \ul conservation of linear momentum}}.\
		A spinning disk 
\f3\i will not
\f1\i0  radiate. This can be regarded as a consequence of the principle of {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Conservation_of_angular_momentum"}}{\fldrslt \cf2 \ul conservation of angular momentum}}. However, it 
\f3\i will
\f1\i0  show {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Gravitoelectromagnetism"}}{\fldrslt \cf2 \ul gravitomagnetic}} effects.\
\pard\pardeftab720\li720\fi-360\sa240\partightenfactor0
\ls3\ilvl0\cf0 		A spherically pulsating spherical star (non-zero monopole moment or {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mass"}}{\fldrslt \cf2 \ul mass}}, but zero quadrupole moment) 
\f3\i will not
\f1\i0  radiate, in agreement with {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Birkhoff%27s_theorem_(relativity)"}}{\fldrslt \cf2 \ul Birkhoff's theorem}}.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sb240\sa240\partightenfactor0
\cf0 More technically, the third time derivative of the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Quadrupole"}}{\fldrslt \cf2 \ul quadrupole moment}} (or the 
\f3\i l
\f1\i0 -th time derivative of the 
\f3\i l
\f1\i0 -th {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Multipole_expansion"}}{\fldrslt \cf2 \ul multipole moment}}) of an isolated system's {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Stress%E2%80%93energy_tensor"}}{\fldrslt \cf2 \ul stress\'96energy tensor}} must be non-zero in order for it to emit gravitational radiation. This is analogous to the changing dipole moment of charge or current that is necessary for the emission of {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Electromagnetic_radiation"}}{\fldrslt \cf2 \ul electromagnetic radiation}}.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sb280\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f0\b\fs28 \cf0 Binaries[{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://en.wikipedia.org/w/index.php?title=Gravitational_wave&action=edit&section=5"}}{\fldrslt \cf2 \ul edit}}]
