 \
Two stars of dissimilar mass are in {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Circular_orbits"}}{\fldrslt \cf2 \ul circular orbits}}. Each revolves about their common {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Center_of_mass"}}{\fldrslt \cf2 \ul center of mass}} (denoted by the small red cross) in a circle with the larger mass having the smaller orbit.\
 \
Two stars of similar mass are in circular orbits about their center of mass\
 \
Two stars of similar mass are in highly {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Elliptical_orbit"}}{\fldrslt \cf2 \ul elliptical orbits}} about their center of mass\
\pard\pardeftab720\sb240\sa240\partightenfactor0
\cf0 Gravitational waves carry energy away from their sources and, in the case of orbiting bodies, this is associated with an inspiral or decrease in orbit.{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Gravitational_wave#cite_note-45"}}{\fldrslt \cf2 \super \ul [}}\cf2 \super \ul 45]{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Gravitational_wave#cite_note-46"}}{\fldrslt [}}46]\cf0 \nosupersub \ulnone  Imagine for example a simple system of two masses \'97 such as the Earth\'96Sun system \'97 moving slowly compared to the speed of light in circular orbits. Assume that these two masses orbit each other in a circular orbit in the 
\f3\i x
\f1\i0 \'96
\f3\i y
\f1\i0  plane. To a good approximation, the masses follow simple Keplerian {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Orbit"}}{\fldrslt \cf2 \ul orbits}}. However, such an orbit represents a changing {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Quadrupole#Gravitational_quadrupole"}}{\fldrslt \cf2 \ul quadrupole moment}}. That is, the system will give off gravitational waves.\
In theory, the loss of energy through gravitational radiation could eventually drop the Earth into the Sun. However, the total energy of the Earth orbiting the Sun ({\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Kinetic_energy"}}{\fldrslt \cf2 \ul kinetic energy}} + {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Gravitational_potential_energy"}}{\fldrslt \cf2 \ul gravitational potential energy}}) is about 1.14\'d710\super 36\nosupersub  {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Joules"}}{\fldrslt \cf2 \ul joules}} of which only 200 {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Watt"}}{\fldrslt \cf2 \ul Watts}} (joules per second) is lost through gravitational radiation, leading to a {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Orbital_decay"}}{\fldrslt \cf2 \ul decay in the orbit}} by about 1\'d710\super \uc0\u8722 15\nosupersub  meters per day or roughly the diameter of a {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Proton"}}{\fldrslt \cf2 \ul proton}}. At this rate, it would take the Earth approximately 1\'d710\super 13\nosupersub  times more than the current {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Age_of_the_Universe"}}{\fldrslt \cf2 \ul age of the Universe}} to spiral onto the Sun. This estimate overlooks the decrease in 
\f3\i r
\f1\i0  over time, but the majority of the time the bodies are far apart and only radiating slowly, so the difference is unimportant in this example.\
More generally, the rate of orbital decay can be approximated by{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Gravitational_wave#cite_note-Gravitational_Radiation-47"}}{\fldrslt \cf2 \super \ul [}}\cf2 \super \ul 47]\cf0 \nosupersub \ulnone \
\pard\pardeftab720\sb160\sa160\partightenfactor0
